it's the first christmas night  having traveled hundreds of miles for  many days  three wise kings from the east have come  to bethlehem  following a bright star in the sky they  know this star signifies the coming of  the new king of israel  in a small barn a family joseph mary and  their new baby jesus are surrounded by  shepherds and their animals who have  just witnessed the miracle of jesus  birth  the three kings come to join the group  and offer gifts of gold frankincense and  myrrh  and they pay homage to jesus  recognizing him as christ the messiah  at least that's the story we are told on  christmas day but this picture is not  quite right  according to the original tale told in  the gospel of matthew the three wise men  didn't come until after jesus was born  traditionally it is thought that they  showed up after 12 days  hence the 12 days of christmas  also the story of the shepherds  worshiping jesus isn't mentioned in the  gospel of matthew but in the gospel of  luke  and that does happen on the night of  jesus's birth  the combination of these events in the  traditional nativity scene is a  convenient artistic convention  okay what else is wrong  well  how many wise men were there  the gospel account states now when jesus  was born in bethlehem of judea  in the days of herod the king behold  there came wise men from the east to  jerusalem there is no mention of the  number of wise men  in the western christian tradition it's  assumed there are three matching the  number of gifts  which seems reasonable to me  but in many parts of the eastern  christian world there are thought to  have been 12 wise men  okay  the wise men didn't show up on christmas  day and we don't know how many there  were  but hang on there's one more problem  since when were they called kings  [Music]  while the three gift givers in the  christmas story are often called kings  there is no indication that they are  rulers of any kind in the gospel  narrative  the supposition of their royalty started  appearing in the 3rd or 4th centuries  experts assumed they started to be  referred to as kings because of passages  in the old testament specifically in the  books of isaiah and psalms the gentiles  shall come to your light and the kings  to the brightness of your rising  the kings of tarshish and of the isles  shall bring presents  the kings of sheba and seba  shall bring gifts  this characterization of them as kings  continued into the medieval period and  in many respects continues today the  kings were even given names and  countries of origin which they  presumably were rulers of depending on  the tradition their names changed but  the western church eventually settled on  melchior  casper and balthazar  malchior is usually depicted as an old  white-haired king of persia  casper is middle-aged and is usually  said to be king of india and lastly  balthazar is a young dark-skinned man  and a king of arabia  though in later traditions is commonly  said to be an ethiopian king  i should point out that all of this is  just the most common interpretation  what the king's names are their ages and  places of origins are all pretty  malleable because well it's all made up  well all of these conventions and  traditions are interesting it falls on  me to point out that they are all wrong  or at the very least completely  unfounded for one the men weren't kings  clearly they were men of some importance  as they do visit king herod before  visiting jesus and not just anyone could  get an audience with the king after all  but the gospel doesn't say that they are  royalty of any kind secondly they aren't  from separate kingdoms when they leave  judea the bible says  they departed for their own country  singular another way  so okay who were these men  well it's actually not hard to figure  out  in most english translations of the  gospel the men are referred to as wise  men  but if we look to the original greek we  see the word used is magi  [Music]  magi was the name given to zoroastrian  priests  zoroastrianism was a major religion in  the ancient world practiced mostly in  persia which at this point was part of  the parthian empire  its founder the prophet zoroaster  sometimes called zarathustra taught that  there was one supreme god named ahara  mazda meaning wise lord and one evil  entity named angra manu  these two deities were said to be in a  constant lock struggle  good versus evil and the message of  zoroaster and his later followers was to  always take the side of good of ahara  mazda  as you can probably see this sort of  cosmology was very influential to  christian theology and helped promote  the devil a character rarely seen or  even discussed in judaism and make him a  central figure in christianity  anyway in the greek speaking world magi  were quite well respected as sages and  above all else astrologers which makes  sense as the biblical magi were said to  have followed a star to jesus  this respect for magi may well be why  they are included in the gospel account  though there is probably more to it than  that  see the writers of the gospels were very  well acquainted with the old testament  scriptures and made constant references  to them  this story in the gospel of matthew is  making reference to the book of numbers  the fourth book of the hebrew bible in  numbers there is a character called  balam  he is a priest and a diviner from persia  hired by the moabites a tribe of people  blocking the jews from the promised land  he is ordered to set a curse on the  israelites but ballan doesn't do that  after he has a vision from the god of  abraham balaam blesses the israelites  instead and gives a prophecy  i see him but not now i behold him but  not near a star shall come out of jacob  a scepter shall rise out of israel so  the author of the gospel of matthew is  asserting that these magi are coming to  fulfill that prophecy  also many medieval theologians like  saint ambrose linked the magi with  balaam they saw the zoroastrian magi as  coming from the same tradition as balaam  in fact some christians claimed that  balaam and zoroaster were actually one  in the same  though this was not a majority opinion  so those were the wise men  but what about those gifts  [Music]  the wise men were said to give gifts of  gold frankincense and myrrh to the holy  family  gold everyone understands  but what are frankincense and myrrh and  what do they mean  well on a surface level like gold they  were very valuable products  frankincense is a natural resin that was  usually used to make incense and myrrh  is similarly a natural resin but was  more commonly used to make essential  oils  basically they were both very precious  materials used by the wealthy but the  gifts may have had symbolic meaning as  well  the gold was meant to represent christ's  kingliness frankincense which was used  by the jews in religious ritual was  meant to represent christ's godliness  and finally myrrh which was often used  in embalming and to prepare dead bodies  for their funerals represented christ's  mortality an eventual sacrifice  traditionally it was said that melchior  gifted the frankincense balthazar gifted  the myrrh and casper gifted the gold but  again none of that is in the bible  account  whether there were three wise men or  twelve whether they were kings or  astrologers or priests what really  matters is that their story gave us my  favorite christmas carol  see you next time and happy holidays
 

